            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Amarna: A New Capital</strong><br>\nNefertiti and her husband established a new capital city called Akhetaten, known today as Amarna. The city was built to honor Aten and served as the center of their religious movement. This period, known as the Amarna Period, was when Nefertiti flourished as a queen and had a substantial influence on the arts and culture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nefertiti's Representation in Art</strong><br>\nArt from the Amarna Period was distinctive and broke from the rigid style of previous Egyptian art. Nefertiti is perhaps best known for her bust, discovered in 1912, which has become an icon of feminine beauty and power. The art from this period emphasized a more naturalistic and intimate portrayal of the royal family.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nefertiti's Family Life</strong><br>\nNefertiti gave birth to six daughters and possibly had a son. The fate of her children is intertwined with the dynastic struggles and intrigues of the time. Some of her daughters married into the royal family, while others were prominent in the religious life of Amarna.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
